  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The stretch of dry and quiet weather will persist across \nthe region as high pressure continues to build into the region, with \nthe center of the high overhead tomorrow into Sunday before \ngradually shifting eastward. Ideal radiational cooling conditions \nwill allow for some cold overnight lows tonight, with temperatures \ndropping into the teens and 20s, likely a few degrees cooler than \nthe last few nights. Temperatures will gradually moderate throughout \nthe weekend, with high temperatures warming into the lower 60s for \nSunday and Monday. Dewpoints will be fairly low, dropping into the \n20s or so, but no fire weather concerns are anticipated given the \nlack of winds across the region with the high pressure overhead. A \nweak cold front looks to move through the region on Monday, but with \nsuch dry air ahead of the feature and lack of moisture, no \nprecipitation is expected just a slight increase in cloud cover.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Our prolonged period of dry weather will continue \nthrough at least the middle part of next week as sprawling high \npressure returns on Tuesday. Longer range models continue to show \nanother cold frontal passage late week, accompanied by light QPF. \nGiven the lack of a deep plume of moisture ahead of this front, \nwouldn't be surprised if we get nothing but a period of increased \ncloud cover, much like our frontal passage yesterday. Still, it's a \nweek out, so have stayed with the NBM/WPC forecast for now, which \ngives low chances of showers late next week. Temperatures will \nremain seasonable, coolest on Tuesday but warming as the week \nprogresses.",
